course: everyday
lesson: 518
title: When a parent has died
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When a parent dies, they don't come back; that is the hardest truth there is. The sadness that follows is called grief, and it doesn't go in a straight line. On Mother's Day Mollie's class makes cards. Her teacher kneels by her desk and says quietly, «You could make one for your mum anyway, or for your nan. Whatever feels right.» Mollie makes one for Mum, with a sunflower because Mum loved them, and at home she stands it by Mum's photo. She feels sad, and glad she didn't have to pretend.
explain: Grief turns up in everyday moments. At recess a kid brags, «My dad's taking me to the Bears game Sunday!» (the Bears are an American football team.) Darnell goes quiet; his dad used to take him. His buddy Marco just says, «Yo, come with us, my uncle's got an extra ticket, no cap.» («No cap» means no lie, for real.) Darnell is sad, then grateful. Lorcan breaks pencils and kicks the bins for weeks. His teacher asks him to stay back. «Ye alright, Lorcan?» He bursts into tears. Grief can look like anger, sadness, tiredness, even giggling at the wrong moment. All of it is normal, and talking helps; charities such as Winston's Wish help grieving children in the UK.
explain: Many grieving children are scared of forgetting. One night Elin realises she can't remember Mam's voice. She panics. Dad digs out an old video of Mam singing at a barbecue, badly, and they watch it together and cry and laugh. Now Elin has a memory box: the video, Mam's bus pass, a shell. Maeve finds Mam's perfume and sprays it on her pillow; it smells like a hug. Freya-Lou's mum died when she was a baby. When friends ask, «Do you miss her?», she says, «I don't know. I never knew her.» Both missing and not knowing how to miss are okay. Her dad tells her stories so she can know her a bit.
explain: Kwame is sick of hearing «Sorry for your loss». He says, «Thanks,» and changes the subject; people mean well, they just don't know what else to say. You're allowed to say, «Thanks, can we talk about something else?» Tiana tries to be extra good so Mum won't be sadder: tidy room, no fuss, all smiles. One night Mum finds her crying under the duvet. «Oh, sweetheart, you don't have to look after me. We can be sad together.» Children don't have to protect a grieving parent.
explain: The first Christmas without Da, Cian's family hangs Da's favourite bauble, a wonky robin, right at the front of the tree, and they tell the story of him falling off the ladder in 2019. Cian laughs and cries at once. At her new school a girl asks Ruby-Ann, «What does your maw do?» («maw» is Scots for mum). Ruby-Ann says, «She died.» «Oh. Sorry.» It's awkward, and then it passes; a short answer is enough. Bodhi's dad loved the beach. Since he died, Bodhi is scared of the ocean. One arvo (afternoon, in Australia) his mum takes him just to look. The next week he paddles. Grief doesn't vanish, but bit by bit it gets easier to carry.
fact: When someone dies, they do not come back; the sadness people feel after a death is called grief.
fact: Grief can come out as sadness, anger, tiredness, fear or even laughing; all of these are normal.
fact: Special days like Mother's Day or Father's Day can hurt; making a card for the parent who died, or for someone else who cares for you, is fine.
fact: Friends help a grieving child most by including them, not by avoiding them.
fact: Photos, videos, stories, smells and memory boxes help children remember a parent who died.
fact: A child may not remember a parent who died when they were very young; missing them or not knowing what you feel are both okay.
fact: People say «Sorry for your loss» because they mean well; you can thank them and change the subject.
fact: Children don't have to stay extra good or happy to protect a grieving parent; families can be sad together.
fact: First holidays without someone are hard; old traditions and funny stories help.
fact: A short answer like «She died» is enough when someone asks; you don't owe more.
fact: Fear after a loss is normal, and small, slow steps help.
fact: Talking to a trusted adult helps, and charities such as Winston's Wish support grieving children.
fact: «No cap» means no lie, «maw» is Scots for mum, and «arvo» is Australian for afternoon.
exercise: What is grief? | The sadness people feel after someone dies | A kind of illness that you can catch at school | Being cross with someone who borrowed your stuff | A feeling that only ever lasts for one day
exercise: What did Mollie's teacher quietly say on Mother's Day? | She could make a card for Mum anyway, or for Nan | She should sit out and read a book until it was over | She had to make a card for the teacher instead | She should pretend her mum was still alive
exercise: Where did Mollie put her card? | By Mum's photo | In the bin | In her teacher's bag | Under her bed
exercise: How did Marco help Darnell? | Invited him to the game | Told him to stop being sad | Avoided him for a week | Changed the subject to football scores
exercise: Marco says, «my uncle's got an extra ticket, no cap». What does «no cap» mean? | No lie, for real | He has no hat | There's no limit | The ticket is free
exercise: Lorcan breaks pencils and kicks bins. What's going on? | Grief can show up as anger | He's just a naughty boy who needs a punishment | He wants new pencils and can't wait for them | He's practising for the football team on Saturday
exercise: Elin forgot Mam's voice and panicked. What helped? | Watching an old video with Dad | Trying hard never to think about Mam | Asking a stranger to do Mam's voice for her | Throwing away the photos so it hurt less
exercise: What is in Elin's memory box? | A video, Mam's bus pass and a shell | Nothing, it's empty | Only her homework | Dad's car keys
exercise: Why did Maeve spray Mam's perfume on her pillow? | The smell made her feel close to Mam | She wanted to cover up a smell of dog | Her pillow was wet from the rain | Dad told her to use it all up quickly
exercise: Freya-Lou says, «I don't know. I never knew her.» Is that okay? | Yes, not knowing what you feel is okay | No, every child must miss their mum the same way | No, she should make up memories so she fits in | Yes, but only if she never asks about her mum again
exercise: Kwame is sick of «Sorry for your loss». What can he say? | «Thanks, can we talk about something else?» | «Shut up, you don't even care about me» | Nothing, he must listen every single time | «It's fine, it doesn't matter to me»
exercise: Why was Tiana trying to be extra good? | So Mum wouldn't be sadder | To get extra pocket money | Because her teacher told her to | So she could go to bed later
exercise: What did Mum tell Tiana? | They could be sad together | She must keep smiling for her | She should tidy her room more | She was too old to cry
exercise: Why did Cian's family put Da's bauble at the front of the tree? | To include him in Christmas and remember him | Because it was the only bauble they had left | Because the robin was the shiniest one | To hide a gap where the tree was bare
exercise: «What does your maw do?» What is a «maw»? | Mum, in Scots | A strict teacher | A corner shop | A pet dog
exercise: How did Bodhi get less scared of the ocean? | Small steps: looking first, paddling later | Being thrown straight in by his big cousin | Never going near any water again at all | Watching shark films every night for a week
test: «my cousin says if u wish hard enough ur mum comes back». Right? | No, people who die don't come back | Yes, if you wish on your birthday | Yes, but only on Mother's Day | No, but she might send a sign on your birthday
test: «Lorcan's well moody, he kicked the bin again, what's his problem». What's most likely? | He's grieving, and it comes out as anger | He's just a naughty kid who loves kicking stuff | He's bored at school and wants to go home early | He doesn't care about anybody or anything now
test: Darnell's quiet when kids talk about dads and games. What would most likely help? | Including him, like Marco did | Never mentioning dads near him again, ever | Telling him to cheer up, it was ages ago | Leaving him alone at recess till he's okay
test: «crap, i can't remember Mum's laugh, i'm the worst». What could help most? | Videos, photos and family stories | Trying never to think of her | Telling no one about it | Pretending you remember
test: In a cartoon, a sad kid is told «don't cry, be brave» and is fine the next day. What's more real? | Crying is okay, and grief takes a long time | Brave kids never cry about anything | Grief goes away overnight if you're brave | Cartoons show grief exactly as it is
test: What would most likely happen if Tiana kept pretending to be happy for months? | She'd feel lonelier and more tired | Mum would stop being sad for good | Her grief would disappear on its own | Nobody would ever notice anything
test: A new kid asks Ruby-Ann about her maw. Ruby-Ann says, «She died.» Does she have to say more? | No, a short answer is enough | Yes, she must tell the whole story | Yes, or it's rude to the new kid | No, but she must say it's fine
test: Kwame's mate says «sorry for ur loss bro» for the tenth time. Why do people keep saying it? | They mean well and don't know what else to say | They are trying to make Kwame feel even worse | Teachers make them say it every morning | It's the only thing people are allowed to say
test: ★★★ Freya-Lou was 6 months old when her mum died. She is 9 now. How many years has it been, rounded to the nearest half year? | 8 and a half | 9 | 9 and a half | 8
test: ★★★ Bodhi looked at the sea on Saturday. He paddled exactly one week later, and swam two weeks after that. On what day of the week did he swim? | Saturday | Sunday | Friday | Monday
test: Elin says, «if I cry about Mam, Dad'll be sad, so I won't». What would Dad most likely say? | We can be sad together | Yes, never cry at home | Only cry at school then | Crying is for babies
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises grief in children? | It may manifest as sadness, anger, fatigue or fear, all of which are normal | It manifests solely as weeping and ceases within a single week | It is a contagious illness requiring medical isolation | It is experienced exclusively by adults and never by children
exam: Identify the conduct most likely to assist a pupil who has lost a parent. | Inclusion of the pupil in ordinary activities | Avoidance of the pupil until recovery | Instruction to the pupil to cease grieving | Repeated reminders of the date of death
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the preservation of memories of a deceased parent. | Photographs, recordings, stories and keepsakes assist remembrance | The removal of all photographs hastens recovery and is advised | Remembrance is harmful and ought to be discouraged in the home | Memories are best preserved by refraining from all discussion
exam: Given that a child is asked by a stranger to explain a parent's death, ascertain the extent of explanation required. | A brief statement suffices, and no further detail is owed | A complete account of all circumstances must be furnished | The child is obliged to respond in writing within a week | The child must decline to acknowledge the parent's death
